<title>SINGAPORE: Singapore computer group shrugs off EU chip move.</title>
<headline>Singapore computer group shrugs off EU chip move.</headline>
<dateline>SINGAPORE 1997-03-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>The chairman of the Singapore Federation of the Computer Industry (SFCI) said on Tuesday the European Commission's (EU) decision to reintroduce minimum prices for DRAM computer chips from South Korea and Japan should not affect manufacturers in Southeast Asia.</p>
<p>&quot;I think it is quite specific to Japan and Korea. We are not aware that this is going to be levied on ASEAN,&quot; said William Liu, chairman of SFCI, a private industry group.</p>
<p>Asked if the move would affect producers of DRAM chips in the region, he told Reuters: &quot;At this point in time, I don't believe so.&quot; ASEAN, the Association of South East Asian Nations, is a leading exporter of computer and electronic products. It groups Brunei, Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and Vietnam.</p>
<p>The EU said the minimum prices would be introduced gradually to avoid harming the EU chip market where prices for DRAMS are low.</p>
<p>An EU official said the market for DRAM, or dynamic random access memory, chips was worth five billion European currency units (US$5.7 billion) in 1995.</p>
<p>-- Singapore Newsroom (65-8703305)</p>
</text>
